<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercisegroup xml:id="exercise-identifying-partial-and-total-orders" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Identifying partial and total orders
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Determine whether or not the given preorder is a partial order. If the preorder is a partial order, determine whether or not it is total.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> R </m> be the preorder on the set <m> \set{ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 } </m> defined by
        
        <md>
          \set{ \of{ 4 , 2 } , \of{ 4 , 3 } , \of{ 2 , 1 } , \of{ 3 , 1 } , \of{ 4 , 1 } , \of{ 1 , 3 } , \of{ 2 , 3 } , \of{ 4 , 4 } , \of{ 3 , 3 } , \of{ 2 , 2 } , \of{ 1 , 1 } }
        </md>.
        
        Determine whether or not <m> R </m> is a partial order on <m> \set{ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 } </m>. If so, determine whether or not it is total.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m> R </m> is not a partial order.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We observe that <m> \of{ 1 , 3 } , \of{ 3 , 1 } \in R </m>, but <m> 1 \neq 3 </m>. Thus <m> R </m> is not anti-symmetric, and so <m> R </m> is not a partial order.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \precsim </m> be the preorder on the set <m> P </m> of all people (living or dead) defined so that for all people <m> p , q \in P </m>, <m> p \precsim q </m> if and only if either <m> p </m> is either <m> q </m> or an ancestor of <m> q </m>. Determine whether or not <m> \precsim </m> is a partial order on <m> P </m>. If so, determine whether or not it is total.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m> \precsim </m> is a partial order but not a total order.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> p , q \in P </m> be people, and suppose that <m> p \preceq q </m> and <m> q \preceq p </m>. If <m> p </m> and <m> q </m> are different people, then it is not possible for each to be the ancestor of the other (since ancestors are born strictly before their descendants). Thus <m> p </m> and <m> q </m> are the same person. We conclude that the preorder <m> \precsim </m> is anti-symmetric and therefore a partial order.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        However, <m> \precsim </m> is not a total order, since for example my siblings and I are not comparable. For example, my sister is not my ancestor, and I am not my sister's ancestor.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \precsim </m> be the preorder on the set <m> C </m> of all circles in the Cartesian plane so that for all circles <m> c , d \in C </m>, <m> c \precsim d </m> if and only if the diameter of <m> c </m> is at most the diameter of <m> d </m>. Determine whether or not <m> \precsim </m> is a partial order on <m> C </m>. If so, determine whether or not it is total.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m> \precsim </m> is not a partial order.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Since there are distinct circles with the same diameter, <m> \precsim </m> is not anti-symmetric, and therefore is not a partial order.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
